// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Music theory explains how music works. It starts with reading notes on the staff.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Music is written on a staff of five lines and four spaces. Clefs set the pitches: the treble clef (G clef) for higher notes and the bass clef (F clef) for lower notes."},{"t":"key","text":"Treble clef lines (bottom to top): E G B D F (\"Every Good Boy Does Fine\"); spaces: F A C E. Bass clef lines: G B D F A (\"Good Boys Do Fine Always\"); spaces: A C E G (\"All Cows Eat Grass\")."},{"t":"p","text":"The musical alphabet uses A through G, then repeats. Ledger lines extend the staff. Middle C sits between the two staves."},{"t":"p","text":"Accidentals change pitch: a sharp (♯) raises a note by a half step, a flat (♭) lowers it by a half step, and a natural (♮) cancels them."}]},{"say":"Rhythm is the timing of music. Let's learn how notes and beats are measured.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Note values: whole note (4 beats in 4/4), half note (2), quarter note (1), eighth note (½), sixteenth note (¼). Each has a matching rest. A dot adds half the note's value: a dotted half note = 3 beats."},{"t":"p","text":"The time signature tells how many beats are in each measure (top number) and which note gets one beat (bottom number). 4/4 (common time) has four quarter-note beats; 3/4 has three (waltz)."},{"t":"p","text":"Simple meters divide beats into two (2/4, 3/4, 4/4); compound meters divide beats into three (6/8, 9/8, 12/8)."},{"t":"p","text":"Tempo is the speed of the beat, often marked in Italian: largo (slow), andante (walking pace), allegro (fast)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"In 4/4 time, how many eighth notes fill one measure?","steps":["4 beats; each eighth note is ½ beat"],"answer":"8 eighth notes"}]}],[{"say":"Scales are the building blocks of melodies and harmonies. The major scale sounds bright and happy.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The major scale follows the pattern of whole (W) and half (H) steps: W W H W W W H. A half step is the smallest distance (adjacent keys on a piano); a whole step is two half steps."},{"t":"p","text":"C major has no sharps or flats: C D E F G A B C. Other major scales need sharps or flats to keep the pattern."},{"t":"p","text":"Scale degrees have names: 1 tonic, 2 supertonic, 3 mediant, 4 subdominant, 5 dominant, 6 submediant, 7 leading tone."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Build G major.","steps":["Start on G: G A B C D E F♯ G","F needs a sharp to keep the W-H pattern"],"answer":"G major has one sharp: F♯"}]},{"say":"Minor scales sound darker. Every major key has a relative minor that shares its key signature.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The natural minor scale pattern: W H W W H W W. A minor has no sharps or flats (A B C D E F G A)."},{"t":"p","text":"Harmonic minor raises the 7th degree; melodic minor raises the 6th and 7th going up."},{"t":"p","text":"Relative keys share a key signature: the relative minor starts on the 6th degree of the major scale (C major and A minor). Parallel keys share a tonic (C major and C minor)."},{"t":"p","text":"The circle of fifths organizes keys: moving clockwise adds a sharp (C, G, D, A…); counterclockwise adds a flat (C, F, B♭, E♭…). Order of sharps: F C G D A E B. Order of flats: B E A D G C F."}]}],[{"say":"An interval is the distance between two notes. Intervals create the colors of melody and harmony.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Intervals have a number (count the letter names, including both) and a quality: perfect (unisons, 4ths, 5ths, octaves), major or minor (2nds, 3rds, 6ths, 7ths), augmented or diminished."},{"t":"p","text":"Semitone counts: minor 2nd = 1, major 2nd = 2, minor 3rd = 3, major 3rd = 4, perfect 4th = 5, tritone = 6, perfect 5th = 7, minor 6th = 8, major 6th = 9, minor 7th = 10, major 7th = 11, octave = 12."},{"t":"p","text":"Consonant intervals sound stable (3rds, 5ths, 6ths, octaves); dissonant intervals sound tense (2nds, 7ths, the tritone)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"What interval is C up to E?","steps":["Count C-D-E: a 3rd; 4 half steps"],"answer":"A major 3rd"}]},{"say":"Chords are three or more notes played together. Triads are the most basic chords.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A triad stacks two 3rds: root, third and fifth."},{"t":"list","items":["Major triad: major 3rd + minor 3rd (C E G), sounds bright.","Minor triad: minor 3rd + major 3rd (A C E), sounds dark.","Diminished: two minor 3rds (B D F), tense.","Augmented: two major 3rds (C E G♯), unstable."]},{"t":"p","text":"Roman numerals label chords by scale degree: in a major key, I, IV and V are major; ii, iii and vi are minor; vii° is diminished."},{"t":"p","text":"Seventh chords add a 7th above the root. The dominant seventh (V7) is very common and strongly pulls to the tonic."},{"t":"p","text":"Inversions put a note other than the root in the bass: first inversion (third in bass), second inversion (fifth in bass)."}]}],[{"say":"Chord progressions give music direction. Cadences are the punctuation marks that end phrases.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Harmonic function: tonic (I, home), predominant (IV, ii, leading away), and dominant (V, V7, creating tension that wants to resolve to I)."},{"t":"p","text":"Common progressions: I–IV–V–I, I–V–vi–IV (heard in countless pop songs), ii–V–I (jazz)."},{"t":"p","text":"Cadences:"},{"t":"list","items":["Authentic cadence (V–I): strong and final, like a period.","Half cadence (ends on V): unfinished, like a comma.","Plagal cadence (IV–I): the \"amen\" cadence.","Deceptive cadence (V–vi): a surprise."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"A phrase ends on a V chord. What cadence is it?","steps":["It stops on the dominant, unresolved"],"answer":"A half cadence"}]},{"say":"Form is the structure of a piece of music, like the outline of an essay.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Phrases are musical sentences, often 4 or 8 measures long. Two phrases can form a period (antecedent and consequent)."},{"t":"p","text":"Common forms:"},{"t":"list","items":["Binary (AB): two contrasting sections.","Ternary (ABA): statement, contrast, return.","Rondo (ABACA): a recurring theme alternating with contrasting sections.","Theme and variations: a melody altered in each repetition.","Sonata form: exposition, development and recapitulation.","Verse–chorus form: common in popular music."]},{"t":"p","text":"Motives are short musical ideas that are repeated and developed, like the four-note opening of Beethoven's Fifth Symphony."}]}]];
export default text;
